==== When finger training makes sense
#ix("Fingerboard!when to start", main: true)

A #jt("fingerboard") is a board of edges you hang from to train your fingers. After a year of climbing, you will hear advice from "start now" to "wait another year". Both camps have a case.

*The traditional view: build up first.* Eric Hörst#ix("Hörst, Eric") (2011) says the fingerboard is not for true novices. Start only after six months of climbing, with no finger, elbow or shoulder pain, and not while more than about 9 kg overweight #src("G34"). Climbing.com suggests climbing for a year or two first. Novices can hang at low loads, but beginners should not add weight #src("G30"). Lattice puts skill first. It suggests testing a 7-second hang on a 20 mm edge after six months, and prefers long, light hangs to maximal ones #src("G26").

*The newer view: gentle loading can start earlier.* One study used data from 526 users of a training app.#ix("Studies!fingerboard training") Climbers chose their own training, so it isn't proof. Gentle hangs with the feet on the floor gained about as much strength as near-maximal hangs. The gentle sessions took about 10 minutes, with 10-second holds and only "a small strain" in the forearms. Doing both added up; climbing alone gave no gain. The authors warn that maximal hangs may overload novices #src("G28"). In a randomised trial with advanced climbers, hangs at 60% of maximum built less strength than heavier ones, but the authors suggest them for climbers worried about injury #src("G29"). Dave MacLeod#ix("MacLeod, Dave") says circumstances matter more than grade: fingerboarding can suit novices who can't climb often #src("G31"). Lattice coach Ollie Torr#ix("Torr, Ollie") uses gentle hangs even with complete beginners, and keeps one day a week free of finger training #src("G27").

*Where they agree:* nobody suggests heavy hangs with added weight, or campus boarding, one year in.

#keypoint[
  *What fits you* (this book's suggestion): two or three short sessions a week. Hang from a 20 mm edge with your feet on the floor, at "small strain" effort. Use a #tref("half-crimp") or #tref("open-hand") grip, never a full crimp. No added weight and no campus board. Stop if finger pain lasts into the next day. This conditions your tendons. It isn't a shortcut to V5: technique is still your main limit #src("G26").
]

==== Recovery and rest
#ix("Recovery")

Lattice coach Tom Randall#ix("Randall, Tom") finds that past 40 he needs more recovery.#ix("Age!recovery") He has cut back on days with two sessions. On climbing trips he has swapped "two days on, one off" for "one on, one off" or "two on, two off" #src("G35"). A Climbing.com plan for over-40s has two rest days a week #src("G36"). Tendons respond slowly, so treat finger pain the *day after* a session as the signal to back off. After a week or two off, restart easier: tendons lose stiffness within one or two weeks without load #src("G24").

This book's suggestion: three bouldering sessions a week, never more than two days in a row. Make one of them an easy session of drills (@cons-learning).

== Learning technique <cons-learning>

==== What skill looks like

A review of 42 studies describes what skilled climbers do.#ix("Experts and novices!how they move") They pause briefly, take a direct path up the wall and move smoothly. They see more of what each hold offers and waste fewer touches on holds they don't use #src("G43"). Researchers measure smoothness by the #jt("jerk") of the hips (how abruptly they speed up and slow down) #src("G45", "G46").

You can check the same things on video. Do your hips jerk or stall? Is their path direct, or does it wander? How long do you hang still before each move?

==== Principles that work
#ix("Motor learning")

- *Practise with a goal.* #jt("deliberate practice", body: [Deliberate practice]) means a clear goal, quick feedback and tasks you can repeat at a slowly rising level. In sport it explains a real but modest share of skill, about 18% #src("G50"). Give each session one or two named goals, such as #tref("backstep", body: [backsteps]) on the steep wall. Then choose problems that need them. How much you learn depends on how hard a problem is for you, not on its grade #src("G43").
- *Vary what you practise.* #jt("variable practice", body: [Variable practice]) means changing the task a little each time. In one study, climbers on routes with holds that could be gripped two ways explored more. Then they settled into smooth movement. A new route made them explore again #src("G44"). Beginners who only climbed square to the wall improved suddenly once they found side-on positions #src("G45"). Varied practice can make sessions feel worse while you keep more of what you learn #src("G51"). So re-climb problems you've done with a new body position, new feet or a hold banned.
- *Learn where you feel safe.* Fear#ix("Fear") narrows what you notice and try #src("G49"). Try new positions low down on easy problems, not at the top of your hardest one.
- *Think about the hold, not your body.* In research on how people learn movements, an #jt("external focus") usually works better: a cue about the effect of a move, e.g. "push the foothold down into the wall", beats a cue about the body, e.g. "straighten your knee" #src("G52"). We found no climbing study of it. Use the body-part steps in this book to set up a position on the ground. Then climb with one hold-focused cue in mind.
- *Get feedback, but not after every go.*#ix("Feedback") Learners in one study got feedback on their movement after each session #src("G45"). Too much feedback during practice can make you depend on it #src("G51"). Film a few attempts, then watch them.

==== Video, flashing and repeating
#ix("Video analysis")

Film from behind, 3–4 m back, or from the side on steep walls. Watch after two or three attempts. Look for one thing per viewing:

+ Were your hips over your feet before each hand moved?
+ How many times did you readjust a foot?
+ Where did you pause for more than two seconds?
+ Did you swing off like a door (#tref("barn-door"))?
+ Could you hear your feet land?

#ix("Flashing")Reading a problem before you climb it means fewer and shorter stops #src("E34", "G44"). Repeating a problem makes you smoother; new problems make you explore #src("G43", "G44"). So do both in every session (this book's suggestion):

- *Flash block.* Try 3–5 new problems you might #jt("flash") (climb first try). Read each one for a minute or two with the method in Chapter 2. Then climb and compare. A German Alpine Club gym uses this "discuss, climb, compare" exercise in its beginner course #src("G56").
- *Repeat block.* Repeat 2–3 problems you've recently done. Aim for fewer pauses, no foot readjustments and silent feet.

*Watch others.* #ix("Watching others") Novice climbers can pick up new ways to use holds just by watching a video of a skilled climber #src("G47"). Watch where their hips go and which way they face before each move.

==== Drills
#ix("Drills")

Do drills on problems two or three grades below your best #src("G53"). One session a week of 25–40 easy problems works well (this book's suggestion). Lattice argues that easy, continuous climbing teaches relaxed movement #src("G54"). The evidence it cites is about forearm fitness, though, not technique.

