== Falling and landing safely <cons-falling>
#ix("Falling", main: true)

==== Why it matters

Most serious bouldering injuries come from landings, not from climbing.#ix("Injuries!from falls") The #jt("DAV")#ix("DAV") (German Alpine Club) collects reports of gym accidents that needed an ambulance or caused at least a moderate injury. In its 2025 figures, 89% of bouldering accidents were falls onto the mat. In 46% of those falls, an ankle or leg twisted on landing. In 27%, the climber landed on an arm. 17% happened at or near the top hold #src("G12"). A survey of 245 injured boulderers#ix("Studies!injuries from falls") found that:

- 85% of the falls were unplanned. Most came from slipping feet, jumps and missed holds.
- 62% of the falls involved a twist. 74% ended feet first.
- Falls from low on the wall were far less likely to cause a severe injury than falls from the top.
- 94% landed on the mats. The mats were there; the landing went wrong #src("G14").

In a study of knee injuries, every torn #jt("ACL")#ix("ACL") came from an uncontrolled fall #src("G9"). The ACL is the main #jt("ligament") inside the knee: a tough band that holds the joint together. The DAV says climbers must train climbing down, falling and landing #src("G12"). One DAV gym teaches falling in the first session of its beginner course and repeats it every session #src("G56").

==== How to land
#ix("Falling!how to land", main: true)

#fig("considerations-fall-roll.svg", lbl: "fig-cons-fall", caption: [A safe feet-first fall, side view. The dashed figure in frame 1 is the climber still on the wall. (1) Look down, arms in. (2) Land on both feet, knees bending. (3) Sit back. (4) Roll onto a rounded back, knees to chest, chin tucked.])

+ *Look down as you come off.* Find the landing. Spotting it early speeds up your reaction #src("G59").
+ *Push slightly away from the wall.* You won't scrape down it or catch a foot on a hold #src("G10").
+ *Land on both feet at once, knees bent and springy* #src("G57"). Sources disagree on how wide to put your feet: together #src("G11"), or shoulder-width or wider #src("G55", "G57"). This book suggests hip-width. Landing on both feet matters more than the width.
+ *Keep absorbing.* Sit back onto your bum just behind your heels. Then roll backwards onto a rounded back (@fig-cons-fall) #src("G59", "G10").
+ *Tuck your arms in and hold your head.* Bend your arms and keep them close to your body. Tense your neck so your head stays off the mat. German coaches call this the #jt("Babyboxer")#ix("Babyboxer") position #src("G55"). The authors of the fall survey suggest crossing your arms in front and rolling backwards #src("G14").
+ *Never put your hands down behind you.* Don't land on straight arms (@fig-cons-fall-wrong) #src("G10", "G57", "G58"). The fall survey's authors think many elbow injuries#ix("Injuries!elbow") come from bracing with the arms after falling backwards #src("G14").
+ *Don't go limp or rigid.* Limp risks whiplash. Rigid risks damage to #jt("tendon", body: [tendons]) (the cords that join muscle to bone) and ligaments #src("G60").

Not every source agrees about the arms. One guide says to let your bent arms soak up the last of the impact #src("G38"). Petzl allows a judo-style slap on the mat #src("G10"). The injury data favour tucking them in.

#fig("considerations-fall-mistakes.svg", lbl: "fig-cons-fall-wrong", caption: [Two landings to avoid. (a) Straight arms planted behind you load the wrists, elbows and shoulders. (b) Locked knees send the impact into the knees and back.])

==== Climb down rather than jump
#ix("Downclimbing", main: true)

The DAV's rule is to climb down before you jump, using the holds marked for climbing down, and to roll if you need to #src("G11"). Every jump loads your tendons, ligaments and joints. Where your gym marks no descent holds, any holds will do for climbing down #src("G55"). Before you start a problem, check that you can climb down or jump safely. Climb only as high as you are ready to fall #src("G55").

If you're tall, your body starts higher than a short climber's from the same foothold. Every drop is bigger.#ix("Tall climbers!falling") This book's suggestion: climb down until your feet are about 1 m above the mat. Then drop and land as above.

==== Bailing from dynos and big moves
#ix("Bailing!from dynos")

To #jt("bail") is to let go on purpose when a move fails. Jumps cause about a fifth of the falls that injure boulderers #src("G14"). They also cause twisting falls that are hard to control #src("G38"). The DAV recorded climbers landing on or hitting #jt("volume", body: [volumes]) (big hollow shapes bolted to the wall) during jumps #src("G12"). It asks #jt("setter", body: [setters]) (the people who design gym problems) not to put the hardest move at full height, especially a jump to the side#ix("Setting!dangerous jumps") #src("G13"). Before you try a #tref("dyno"), #tref("double-dyno") or #tref("run-and-jump"):

+ *Plan the miss before the go.* Is the landing clear of people, bags and brushes? Is there a volume or big hold below the move that you could hit #src("G12")?
+ *Know how to get out of every hook.* Before a #tref("heel-hook") above your head, ask yourself: would it release if a hand slipped #src("G38")? If not, set the heel lightly or choose another sequence.
+ *Commit fully or don't go.* A half-hearted jump often ends with one hand on and your body swinging. (This book's suggestion.)
+ *If you miss, let go with both hands together.* Bring your arms in. Hanging on with one hand swings you round, so you land twisted. (This book's suggestion. Twisting featured in 62% of falls that caused injury #src("G14").)
+ *If your feet swing off and you can't hold the swing,* let go as your body swings back under the holds. You then drop straight down instead of flying out backwards. See #tref("cutting-loose"). (This book's suggestion.)
+ *Don't flail.* Turning round or spreading your arms in the air makes the landing harder to control #src("G10"). Look down, get your legs under you and land as above.

==== Mats, gaps and the fall zone

- Keep the area you could land in clear.#ix("Mats") No bottles, brushes, chalk bags or people on the mat below a climber. Don't climb right beside or above someone. Judge your landing area generously #src("G11", "G38").
- Watch the mat edges. Landing on an edge or a seam can twist an ankle. Check loose pads after every fall #src("G38"). Still, only 5% of injured boulderers in the fall survey landed in a gap #src("G14").
- Look for things to hit. 3% of the DAV's reported bouldering accidents were impacts with the wall or a volume #src("G12").

==== Spotting indoors
#ix("Spotting")

A #jt("spotting", body: [spotter]) stands below a climber and guides their fall so they land feet first. You guide the climber; you don't catch them #src("G10", "G38"). Stand with your feet apart and knees soft. Bend your arms and hold your hands at hip height. Aim at the climber's hips and upper back. Tuck your thumbs in #src("G10", "G38").

On modern gym floors, the Climbing Wall Association's experts see little need for routine spotting. It doesn't prevent the common ankle and wrist injuries, and an untrained spotter can do harm or get hurt. Spotting still helps in low, steep caves, on #tref("roof", body: [roofs]) and on hooks that could tip you upside down. The experts' favourite advice was to teach falling instead #src("G61"). The DAV says to learn to spot properly, to spot only when needed and to allow for weight differences #src("G11"). If you're tall and heavy, you are hard for a smaller partner to guide #src("G10"). Rely on your own landing.

==== Practise falling
#ix("Falling!practice")

This progression comes from a DAV national instructor #src("G55"):

+ On the mat, from standing: tip backwards into the Babyboxer and roll.
+ Jump up, land on both feet, tip back and roll.
+ With a partner: face each other, hold hands and bend your knees. Lean back against each other's pull. Let go and land in the Babyboxer.
+ On the wall: drop off from low holds. Go gradually higher. Slightly overhanging walls are fine; roofs are not.

Practise often so the right reflexes become automatic. Make a few landings a regular part of your warm-up #src("G57").

#keypoint[
  *Read the fall before you climb.* Four things mark a dangerous fall.#ix("Falling!judging the landing") Look for hard moves near the top hold and heel hooks above your head. Look for sideways jumps and volumes below the hardest move #src("G12", "G14", "G38"). Have you already landed with a stumble, a sideways tip or a hand reaching back? Then practise the roll before you try that move again.
]
